@meta
id: 24-09
title: Join lines by range
chapter: 24
type: lesson
skills: ex-join
par: 20
minutes: 4
vscode: End, Delete, Delete, fix the spacing by hand
open: RUNBOOK.md

@brief
Somebody hard-wrapped the runbook and broke two things. The sentence on
lines 3 to 5 should be one line. The `curl` URL on lines 9 and 10 was
split in the middle of `replay`, and it has to be one line with
nothing inserted at the break, because it is a URL.

`j` is join. With a range it joins all the lines in the range into one,
putting a single space between them and dropping leading whitespace,
which is what you want for prose. `j!` joins without touching anything:
no space, no trimming, the lines simply glued end to end. That is what
you want for a URL, a base64 blob, or anything else where a space is a
bug.

@keys
| `:3,5j` | join lines 3 to 5 into one, with spaces |
| `:9,10j!` | join lines 9 and 10 exactly as they are |
| `:j 3` | join the current line with the two below it |

@hints
Two commands, one for each break. Line numbers in front. Do the sentence
first: after it the file is two lines shorter and the URL has moved.
--
The plain form inserts a space at each join. The bang form inserts
nothing. Read the URL and decide which one it needs.
--
`:3,5j` Enter. The URL is now on lines 7 and 8: `:7,8j!` Enter. `:wq`.

@solution
    :3,5j<Enter>  :7,8j!<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Seventeen keystrokes.

`:3,5j` is `3J` from chapter 8 with a range instead of a count, and
`:j!` is `gJ`. The gain over the normal-mode versions is the same as it
has been all chapter: you do not have to be on the line, and the range
can be anything an address can express. Where the range form really
earns its place is inside `:g`. `:g/^- /j` joins every continuation
line onto its bullet. `:g/,$/j` joins every line that ends in a comma
onto the next. Both are one command for the whole file, and neither is
expressible with `J`.

The trailing count is the other form: `:j 3` joins three lines starting
at the current one, and `:5j 3` starts at line 5. It is the count `3J`
uses, spelled as an argument.

@start RUNBOOK.md
# Refund runbook

When a refund fails the payment service records the attempt
and raises REFUND_FAILED,
which Sentry picks up within a minute.

To replay it, call the gateway directly:

    curl -X POST https://gateway.internal/v2/refunds/re
play?orderId=ord_1002&reason=REFUND_FAILED

Then mark the order as refunded in the admin.

@expected RUNBOOK.md
# Refund runbook

When a refund fails the payment service records the attempt and raises REFUND_FAILED, which Sentry picks up within a minute.

To replay it, call the gateway directly:

    curl -X POST https://gateway.internal/v2/refunds/replay?orderId=ord_1002&reason=REFUND_FAILED

Then mark the order as refunded in the admin.
